-- title:
--   Component projectors on the special fibre of the Raynaud quotient
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a non-zero natural number with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ is a subgroup containing the whole kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (hypothesis `hHp`), with $M/p$ non-zero. Further, `Pl` is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ is a non-unit of `Pl` (`hPl`), whose residue field is of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed; `hj` records that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, so that the two-chart integral models `toBase p Γ hj` are available. Next, $\mathfrak{X}$ is an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` (a Deligne–Rapoport style integral model package at $p$ for the two levels $\Gamma_M$ and $\Gamma_N$, with its zero section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ and degeneracy $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$), $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl` (a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over `base p` with relative group law $\Lambda.L$ and the prescribed identifications of generic and special points), and $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, the corresponding Néron object $O.G \to$ `base p` for $J_H =$ `JH M H`, the degree-zero divisor class group of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. The hypotheses `hrep` and `hrepΛ` assert that the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designations built from $(O.G, O.g)$ and from $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$, each with the unit section of its group law, represent the sub-Picard condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic triviality) for the curves `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` with zero section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, respectively `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` with zero section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   The local base is a ring `Rh`: a Henselian local domain which is a discrete valuation ring, equipped with an algebra structure on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with injective structure map, whose image lies in `Pl` (`hRA`), whose maximal ideal is exactly the set of elements of `Pl`-valuation $<1$ (`hRloc`), and with a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra structure whose structure map has the same kernel (`hres`), so that $\mathbb{Z}/p$ is the residue field of `Rh`. A set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and a unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ are fixed, with `hd`: the image of $d$ in $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ is $p$.
--
--   The *finite-part witness* consists of a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over `Rh` of height $h$ (a tower of finite free cocommutative Hopf algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition bialgebra maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals), an additive map $\Delta$ from the geometric points $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H$, and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ between the Tate modules (sequences $(x_n)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p x_{n+1} = x_n$). The accompanying hypotheses are: $\Delta$ is injective (`hΔinj`); $y \in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ — the subgroup generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes whose section extends over the place — if and only if $y$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point (`hΔlev`); $\Delta$ is equivariant for $\tau \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and any `Rh`-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ inducing it (`hΔgal`); for every set $S'$ and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S'`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$, $U_q$, $\langle d \rangle$) there is a transition-compatible family of bialgebra endomorphisms of $\mathcal{G}$ inducing `genOpH M H S' g` through $\Delta$ (`hΔhecke`, quantified over all such $S'$); $e$ is given coordinatewise by $\Delta$ (`he`), is injective (`heinj`), has as image exactly the sequences with $n$-th term in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$ (`herange`), intertwines the Galois actions on the two Tate modules (`hegal`), has saturated image (`hsat`), and has cokernel $\mathbb{Z}_p$-free of rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$ (`hcoker`); finally every element of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point (`htor`).
--
--   The *Raynaud quotient data* consist of a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over `Rh` of height $h_B$ and a family $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of bialgebra maps, so that precomposition sends points of $\mathcal{G}$ to points of $\mathcal{B}$, subject to: $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ and $h_B = 2h'$ (`hhB`, `hhB2`); compatibility with transitions (`hψt`); the kernel description, namely the image in $\mathcal{B}$ of a level-$v$ point $x$ is trivial precisely when $\Delta(x) \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ (`hψker`); surjectivity on level-$v$ geometric points (`hψsurj`); the reduction statement `hψred`, that if the image of $x$ in $\mathcal{B}$ reduces to the identity (all values of its algebra map are congruent to the counit modulo valuation $<1$) then so does $x$; and `hperiod`, that for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of `Pl` over $\mathbb{Q}$ and $z$ a $p^v$-torsion class of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, any level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$ has image in $\mathcal{B}$ reducing to the identity.
--
--   The *scheme-level pinning* of $\mathcal{G}$ inside $O.G$ consists of a ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to$ `Rh` compatible with the maps to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρh`) and morphisms $\iota_v : \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to O.G$ satisfying: the base compatibility `hιbase`; the induced map to the pullback is a closed immersion (`hιcl`); $\iota_v$ is killed by multiplication by $p^v$ (`hιp`); $\iota_v$ computes the sections attached to $\Delta$ of level-$v$ points (`hιpts`); $\iota_v$ is multiplicative for the relative group law on $B$-points (`hιmul`); compatibility with transitions (`hιt`); realisation of every Hecke generator by a transition-compatible family of bialgebra endomorphisms of $\mathcal{G}$, both on $O.G$ through $\iota$ and on points through $\Delta$ (`hιhecke`, again quantified over all sets of primes); and `hιfin`, which states that the comparison morphism $j_v$ from $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ into the pullback over $\mathrm{Spec}\,$`Rh` of the $p^v$-kernel of $O$ is simultaneously an open and a closed immersion, and that every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of `Rh` is in its image.
--
--   The *operator families* are: transition-compatible bialgebra endomorphisms $u_v$ of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ realising $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,U_p$ through $\iota$ (`hut`, `huι`); transition-compatible bialgebra automorphisms $D_{p,v}$ realising $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,\langle d\rangle$ through $\iota$ (`hDpt`, `hDpι`); the statements `hutor` and `hDptor` that $u$ and $D_p$ preserve the condition $\Delta(x) \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; and descents to $\mathcal{B}$: endomorphisms $u_{B,v}$, automorphisms $D_{B,v}$ and automorphisms $D'_{B,v}$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$, with $u_v \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ u_{B,v}$ (`huB`), $D_{p,v} \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ D_{B,v}$ (`hDB`) and $D'_{B,v} = \mathrm{id} \otimes D_{B,v}$ (`hDB'`).
--
--   The *special fibre data* are: a morphism $\sigma_p : \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb{Z}/p \to$ `base p` factoring the structure maps as in `hσp` and, via a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra structure on the residue field of `Pl`, compatible with `resPt Pl` followed by $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ (`hfac`); morphisms $\iota^p_v : \mathrm{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to$ `pullback O.g σp` with the two projection identities `hιp₁`, `hιp₂`; and two morphisms $q_0, q_1$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb{Z}/p$ from the base change of $O.g$ along $\sigma_p$ to the base change of $\Lambda.f$, each a homomorphism for the base-changed group laws (`hqmul`) and each agreeing with the designated map `O.abqFibre i` under the canonical comparison of the fibre over the residue field of `Pl` with the fibre over $\sigma_p$ (`hqbc`).
--
--   On the level-$(M/p)$ side there is a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{A}$ over `baseRing p` of height $h_\Lambda$ with $h_B = 2 h_\Lambda$ (`hBΛ`), the identification $\sigma_p = \mathrm{Spec}$ of the structure map `baseRing p` $\to \mathbb{Z}/p$ (`hσp'`), and morphisms $\iota'_v$ from $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the level-$v$ algebra of $\mathcal{A}$ base-changed to $\mathbb{Z}/p$ into `pullback Λ.f σp`, which are closed immersions (`hι'cl`) compatible with the base (`hι'base`) and with transitions (`hι't`), multiplicative (`hι'mul`), and which identify the level-$v$ algebra with the $p^v$-kernel of the base-changed law, the comparison morphism to that kernel being an isomorphism (`hι'p`).
--
--   The *splitting data* are morphisms $r^S_i{}_{,v}$ ($i \in \{0,1\}$) from $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the level-$v$ algebra of $\mathcal{B}$ base-changed to $\mathbb{Z}/p$ into `pullback Λ.f σp`, and bialgebra isomorphisms $\kappa^S_v$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ from that algebra onto the tensor square of the corresponding level-$v$ algebra of $\mathcal{A}$, such that: $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v)$ followed by $r^S_{i,v}$ equals $\iota^p_v$ followed by $q_i$ (`hrSfac`); each $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v)$ is an epimorphism of schemes (`hPepi`); $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $(\kappa^S_v)^{-1}$ precomposed with the left, respectively right, tensor inclusion, followed by $\iota'_v$, equals $r^S_{0,v}$, respectively $r^S_{1,v}$ (`hrS0`, `hrS1`); and $\kappa^S$ is compatible with the transitions on both sides (`hκSt`).
--
--   Finally, the *scheme-level Eichler–Shimura identities*: endomorphisms $U$ and $D$ of the base change of $O.g$ along $\sigma_p$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb{Z}/p$ which, under the projection to $O.G$, are the base changes of $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,U_p$ and of $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,\langle d\rangle$ (`hU`, `hD`); endomorphisms $F_{\mathrm{sch}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{sch}}$ of the base change of $\Lambda.f$, where `hFsch` pins $F_{\mathrm{sch}}$ as the Frobenius, namely for every $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra $B$ of characteristic $p$ and every $B$-point $x$, $x$ followed by $F_{\mathrm{sch}}$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathrm{frobenius}\,B\,p)$ followed by $x$ (no hypothesis is imposed on $V_{\mathrm{sch}}$); and an endomorphism $D_\Lambda$ of the base change of $\Lambda.f$ which is a homomorphism for the base-changed group law (`hDΛmul`), subject to $D$ followed by $q_1$ equals $q_1$ followed by $D_\Lambda$ (`hDq1`), the same with $q_0$ (`hDq0`), and $U$ followed by $q_1$ equals $q_1$ followed by $F_{\mathrm{sch}}$ and then $D_\Lambda$ (`hUq1`).
--
--   Under all of these hypotheses there exist a family $r$ of morphisms $r_{i,v}$ ($i \in \{0,1\}$, $v \in \mathbb{N}$) from $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the level-$v$ algebra of $\mathcal{B}$ base-changed to $\mathbb{Z}/p$ into `pullback Λ.f σp`, and two families $\varepsilon_v, \varepsilon'_v$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bialgebra endomorphisms of that level-$v$ algebra, such that:
--
--   (i) for all $i$ and $v$, $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v)$ followed by $r_{i,v}$ equals $\iota^p_v$ followed by $q_i$;
--
--   (ii) for all $v$, $\mathrm{Spec}(\varepsilon_v)$ followed by $r_{1,v}$ is the trivial morphism, that is, the structure morphism $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the level-$v$ algebra $\to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb{Z}/p$ followed by the unit section of the base-changed law $\Lambda.L$;
--
--   (iii) for all $v$, $\mathrm{Spec}(\varepsilon_v)$ followed by $r_{0,v}$ equals $r_{0,v}$;
--
--   (iv) for all $v$, $\mathrm{Spec}(\varepsilon'_v)$ followed by $r_{0,v}$ is the trivial morphism in the same sense;
--
--   (v) for all $v$, $\mathrm{Spec}(\varepsilon'_v)$ followed by $r_{1,v}$ equals $r_{1,v}$;
--
--   (vi) $\varepsilon_v \circ \varepsilon_v = \varepsilon_v$ and $\varepsilon'_v \circ \varepsilon'_v = \varepsilon'_v$ for all $v$;
--
--   (vii) as $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra maps, both composites $\varepsilon_v \circ \varepsilon'_v$ and $\varepsilon'_v \circ \varepsilon_v$ equal the trivial endomorphism `Algebra.ofId` composed after the counit algebra map;
--
--   (viii) in the convolution monoid of algebra endomorphisms, the product of $\varepsilon_v$ and $\varepsilon'_v$ is the identity map, for every $v$;
--
--   (ix) both families are compatible with the transitions of $\mathcal{B}$ base-changed to $\mathbb{Z}/p$: $\mathrm{transition}_v \circ \varepsilon_{v+1} = \varepsilon_v \circ \mathrm{transition}_v$, and likewise for $\varepsilon'$;
--
--   (x) for all $v$, $\varepsilon_v \circ (\mathrm{id} \otimes u_{B,v}) \circ \varepsilon_v = \varepsilon_v \circ (\mathrm{id} \otimes u_{B,v})$;
--
--   (xi) for all $v$, $\varepsilon_v \circ D'_{B,v} = D'_{B,v} \circ \varepsilon_v$.
--
--   This is the step that splits the special fibre of the Raynaud quotient $\mathcal{B}$ of the $p$-divisible group attached to the Néron object of $J_H$ at $p$ into two copies of the $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{A}$ of the level-$(M/p)$ object, by a transition-compatible pair of orthogonal bialgebra idempotents whose convolution product is the identity, with respect to which $U_p$ is triangular and the diamond operator diagonal. It is used in [`ModularCurve.exists_idempotent_pair_baseChange_raynaudQuotient_projector_components_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_idempotent_pair_baseChange_raynaudQuotient_projector_components_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins), in the analysis at $p$ of the Jacobian $J_H$ underlying level lowering.
